1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,510
Twelve hundred books a month.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:04,010
Twenty-two thousand titles.

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:06,850
Every author question, answered by hand.

4
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:11,750
I built BookLeaf's new author support system.

5
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:13,870
Here's how, and why.

6
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:15,990
The obvious move?

7
00:00:16,380 --> 00:00:18,610
Bolt on AI to type replies faster.

8
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:22,490
But a confident wrong number is worse than a slow reply.

9
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:25,590
So I reframed it.

10
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:28,350
Correct answers in front of a human, fast.

11
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:29,950
Money issues first.

12
00:00:30,220 --> 00:00:32,850
And a desk that never stops when the AI does.

13
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:35,110
Three rules.

14
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:38,470
Code computes the facts, AI only writes the words.

15
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:40,330
A human always sends.

16
00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:42,650
And tickets never depend on the AI.

17
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,750
Then the big idea: don't make authors leave their dashboard.

18
00:00:48,440 --> 00:00:52,190
Support became a module that plugs into any product, for any company.

19
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:55,410
BookLeaf is just workspace number one.

20
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:02,690
Under the hood: one TypeScript monorepo, four apps, and one shared set of Zod schemas.

21
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:08,170
Fastify and Postgres with row-level security.

22
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:09,790
React and Tailwind.

23
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:11,930
Claude for triage and drafts.

24
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:14,210
OpenAI Realtime for voice.

25
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:16,990
Real databases in every test.

26
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:26,170
Every ticket flows through a safe pipeline: triage, real data, an AI draft, then a checker that verifies every amount and date.

27
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:35,890
And I measured it: forty-five of forty-six tickets classified right, Hindi and Hinglish included, at nine cents per thousand.

28
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:44,330
Then I went beyond the brief: chat and voice in one thread, and calls that fill in the ticket while you talk.

29
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:47,110
It wasn't all smooth.

30
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:51,670
Vercel can't carry WebSockets, so calls get a one-time ticket to the API.

31
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:56,890
An audit found cross-company leaks, so I fixed them and added row-level security.

32
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:05,810
Quality was a habit: twelve hundred and forty tests, all green, on real databases.

33
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:09,730
It's live on AWS and Vercel.

34
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,010
Deploys only when I ask, backups first, and one command tears it all down.

35
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:16,950
About eighteen dollars a month.

36
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:22,710
Next: a bigger real test set, one-click approvals, and email alerts.

37
00:02:24,400 --> 00:02:29,490
From a support portal, to a support module any product can plug in.

38
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:30,730
Thanks for watching.
